// The Eagle: the transporter of Moonbase Alpha, from Space: 1999, built part by part from production-style
// drawings and the published particulars that go with them.
//
//     import 'eagle' { Eagle }
//
//     object Eagle()                          // a transporter, 100 ft (30.48 m) from engine bell to nose
//     object Eagle(30.48, 'laboratory')       // carrying the laboratory pod instead
//     object Eagle(30.48, 'none')             // the bare spine, no pod at all
//     object Eagle(30.48, 'transporter', 1)   // in flight, its legs hanging free
//     object Eagle(30.48, 'transporter', 1, 1)   // in flight, its legs stowed
//
// | `Eagle` | The Eagle: command module, spine, sections, leg pods and engines, and the pod it carries. |
// | `EagleLaunchPad` | A launch pad of Moonbase Alpha: its flight deck and elevator, landing lights, terminal and boarding tube. |
// | `EagleLegTravel`, `EagleLength`, `EagleFullLength` | The numbers a landing is keyed by. |
//
// **The first number is its length**, 100 ft (30.48 m) unless said: the size at which the people the drawings
// show fit it -- the cutaway's crewman stands a fifth of the way up its side, and the passenger module's ceiling
// comes to two meters.  **The second is the pod slung under its spine**, by name: `'transporter'`, the passenger
// module and the Eagle unless said; `'rescue'`, the same module banded red and white as a rescue Eagle's is;
// `'laboratory'`, the science pod with its airlocks out to either side; `'freighter'`, a cargo pallet; or `'none'`,
// nothing -- as any name it does not know is.  **The third is how far its legs are run out**, from 0, standing on them with its
// weight on their shock struts (unless said), to 1, hanging free in flight, the pads 0.15 m lower at the drawings'
// size; their knee links fold and open to follow, solved as joints.  A landing keys it from 1 down to 0 as the pads
// meet the ground, and lowers the craft by the same amount, `EagleLegTravel` times it at the drawings' size.  **The
// fourth is how far its legs are stowed**, from 0, out (unless said), to 1, drawn up into their pods, each pad
// filling the cutout in its pod's underside, its sloped faces lying in the pod's lower slopes.
//
// **It is written in meters**, at the size of the drawings it was taken from, and scaled to the length asked for.
// **It faces `+X`**, as the spacecraft do, port toward `+Z`; **it stands on `y = 0`**, the soles of its landing
// pads, since an Eagle is a thing that lands; and the middle of its length is over `x = 0`.
//
// -- Where the shape comes from -----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Geoffrey Mandel's general layout of 1977 (Technical Schema TS-1075A, "Eagle - General Layout"): an outboard
// profile from starboard, elevations of the bow and the stern, and a top plan drawn as a cutaway at the deck,
// with feet and meter scales and the particulars -- 76 ft (23.16 m) long, 30.2 ft (9.2 m) wide across the leg
// pods, 13.97 ft (4.26 m) high, the passenger module 8.15 m long and 4.55 m wide.  Its three sheets agree with one
// another to a sixth of a percent in scale, about 60.2 pixels to the meter as scanned, and its views are the
// skeleton of everything here.  Where they disagree with one another -- the profile draws the engine bells a
// little taller than the stern does -- the stern, which sees them end on, wins.  None of the drawings is
// reproduced here.
//
// **No size was ever given on screen, and the particulars' is too small for a person.**  At 23.16 m the passenger
// module's ceiling, by Mandel's own figure, is 1.54 m.  A cutaway of the internal arrangement (the "Technical
// Release Office" sheet) stands a crewman beside the craft, about a thirtieth of its length tall, which makes a
// six-foot man of an Eagle about 30.4 m long; other drawings scale it to about 32 and 36 m by their own bars.  So
// the Eagle is drawn here at the particulars' size, which every measurement can be checked against, and grown to
// 100 ft unless a scene says otherwise -- where the ceiling comes to 2.03 m and the sliding doors to 2.6.
//
// The pods other than the passenger module come from photographs of the studio models (Starship Modeler's
// reference page: the laboratory pod from its side and three-quarters, the cargo pallet on the 22-inch model).

// -- Materials ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The hull is white, a warm, slightly gray white, painted.
EagleWhite = material { pigment [0.80, 0.80, 0.78]  specular 0.25  shininess 40 }

// The engine bells are bare metal, steel turned and left dull.
EagleBell = material { metal 'iron'  roughness 0.3 }

// The command module's windows: dark, smoked glass, with the gloss of glass.
EagleGlass = material { pigment [0.025, 0.03, 0.04]  specular 0.9  shininess 250  reflective 0.12  fresnel }

// The gray of the fittings -- hatch rings, the collar the command module stands on -- and the near black of what
// lies within them.
EagleGray = material { pigment [0.45, 0.45, 0.47]  specular 0.3  shininess 30 }
EagleDark = material { pigment [0.06, 0.06, 0.07]  specular 0.2  shininess 20 }

// The equipment packed inside the fore and aft sections' cages, seen between the bracing.
EagleWorks = material { pigment [0.5, 0.5, 0.52]  specular 0.2  shininess 20 }

// The small dark ports among the command module's panels, painted on.
EagleHole = color [0.06, 0.06, 0.07]

// The markings' colors: the Moonbase Alpha emblem's white and deep blue, the black of the leg pods' crosses and the
// freighter's hazard stripes, the warning red of the spine's bands and the rescue Eagle's, and the tanks' orange.
EagleEmblemWhite = color [0.94, 0.94, 0.92]
EagleEmblemBlue = color [0.08, 0.2, 0.55]
EagleBlack = color [0.035, 0.035, 0.04]
EagleRed = color [0.72, 0.07, 0.05]
EagleOrange = color [0.95, 0.45, 0.07]
EagleRedPaint = material { pigment EagleRed  specular 0.25  shininess 40 }
EagleBlackPaint = material { pigment EagleBlack  specular 0.25  shininess 40 }

// The band of black and white checks round each of the spheres between the engines' tanks.
EagleChecks = material {
    pigment checker { color [0.04, 0.04, 0.045], color [0.85, 0.85, 0.83]  scale 0.39  translate Y 0.195 }
    specular 0.25  shininess 40
}

// -- Measurements -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The particulars' length, which everything here is drawn at, and the length it is grown to unless a scene says.
EagleLength = 23.16
EagleFullLength = 30.48

// The four main engines, in a diamond seen from astern: top and bottom on the centerline, the other two out to
// either side; each bell's mouth is at the stern and it is 1.53 m from the mouth to the end of its collar.
EagleBellMouth = -11.58
EagleBellLength = 1.53

// The command module: an egg seen from the side, from its back at 7.55 to its point at the nose, 1.6 times as
// wide as it is tall.
EagleCommandBack = 7.55
EagleCommandMiddle = 2.607
EagleCommandWidening = 1.6

// -- Parts --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// -- Markings ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The outlines of the markings, as Briedis and Munro's drawings and the studio models have them (the rescue Eagle's
// bands from a replica of it), worked out by a script and placed where each is laid on:
//   - the Moonbase Alpha emblem -- "ALPHA" over the Earth and the Moon on an Eagle's figure-eight, "MOONBASE" under
//     them, in a trapezoid -- traced from a black-and-white drawing of it (cited, not reproduced) at a third of a
//     pixel in a drawing 560 wide, its width one and its rows running down; its white a plaque, its black the deep
//     blue; on each side of a pod near either end just under the eave, and on each flank of the command module
//     between its windows near the nose;
//   - each leg pod's black cross, four triangles meeting at its thrusters and white between them along the
//     diagonals;
//   - the freighter's hazard stripes, across the end of each of its end blocks;
//   - the rescue module's red bands, half a meter wide a meter apart, red at either end;
//   - two orange bars across the top of each engine's tank.
EagleEmblemPlaque = path {
    move to 0.4874, 0.3158  line to 0.4937, 0.3155  line to 0.4973, 0.3143  line to 0.4999, 0.3105  line to 0.4998, 0.3069
    line to 0.2464, -0.2949  line to 0.2374, -0.3146  line to 0.2348, -0.3160  line to 0.2312, -0.3166  line to -0.2349, -0.3166
    line to -0.2384, -0.3157  line to -0.2407, -0.3128  line to -0.2463, -0.3003  line to -0.3583, -0.0324  line to -0.3844, 0.0319
    line to -0.4923, 0.2891  line to -0.4981, 0.3033  line to -0.5000, 0.3105  line to -0.4974, 0.3139  line to -0.4938, 0.3155
    line to 0.2437, 0.3166  close
}

